<FieldGuide title="Specimen tracking" description="Why every fungal specimen, photo, record and DNA sequence must stay linked, what the research shows when they don't, and a simple system to keep them matched." eyebrow="FIELD RESEARCH TOOLKIT / SPECIMEN TRACKING" heading="Keep the mushroom <em>with its record.</em>" lead="A DNA sequence is only as trustworthy as the label on the bag it came from. Here’s why that link matters, and how to keep it." tourKey="field-tracking" tour={tour} sources={sources} current="tracking">

<section id="why">

  <h2>Names in databases are often wrong. Specimens let us fix them.</h2>
  <p>When scientists compare a new sequence with the public databases, they’re trusting that each stored sequence carries the right name. A 2006 study of fungal sequences found that trust is often misplaced: it estimated that <strong>about 20% of fungal sequences in the public databases may be misidentified to species</strong>.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">1</a></sup></p>
  <p>Mistakes like that can only be corrected if someone can go back to the mushroom the sequence came from. When every sequence is linked to a kept specimen, a later expert can re-examine it, and names can be revised. A 2013 barcoding project built on herbarium specimens did exactly that: because every sequence was tied to a voucher, the team could correct species names attached to sequences.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">2</a></sup></p>
  <div class="mv-guide-grid">
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>A photo</h3><p>Shows what it looked like. It can’t be sequenced or examined under a microscope.</p></div>
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>A sequence on its own</h3><p>Tells you what the DNA says. If the name on it is wrong, nobody can check.</p></div>
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>Specimen + photos + record + sequence</h3><p>Can be checked, corrected and built on by anyone, years later. That only works if all four stay linked.</p></div>
  </div>
  <p>This matters more every year: a 2025 review found that the use of iNaturalist data in peer-reviewed research grew tenfold in five years,<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">7</a></sup> and a 2026 paper sets out best practices for using it.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">8</a></sup> Records linked to specimens and sequences are the ones researchers can trust most.</p>
</section>

<section id="chain">

  <h2>Link the field number to the observation number.</h2>
  <p>Start with a temporary field number. After posting, add the iNaturalist or Mushroom Observer observation number to the same slip or collection log. Keeping both numbers together lets you match the original photos to the online record and the dried specimen.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">3</a></sup><sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">5</a></sup></p>
  <p class="mv-guide-note"><strong>Example:</strong> You photograph the mushroom with field slip <strong>F-017</strong>. After posting, the observation link ends in <strong>/observations/123456789</strong>. Write <strong>iNaturalist 123456789</strong> beside <strong>F-017</strong> on the slip. Keep that slip with the dried specimen.</p>
  <ol class="mv-guide-steps">
    <li><span class="mv-guide-when">In the field</span><h3>Number it before it goes in the basket</h3><p>A field slip (or any pre-numbered tag) goes with the collection, in its own container or wrap. Note the number on your phone or slip with anything you notice: smell, host tree, colour changes.</p></li>
    <li><span class="mv-guide-when">In the field</span><h3>Put the number in the photos</h3><p>Photograph the slip beside the mushroom. Now the photos and the specimen share a number, even before anything is uploaded.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">3</a></sup></p></li>
    <li><span class="mv-guide-when">After posting</span><h3>Write both numbers together</h3><p>Open the posted observation or copy its share link. On iNaturalist, the digits after <code>/observations/</code> are the observation number. Write it next to the field number before moving on to the next collection.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">5</a></sup></p></li>
    <li><span class="mv-guide-when">When drying</span><h3>Label it on the rack</h3><p>Keep the numbered slip corner or a label beside the specimen. If you haven’t posted yet, start drying with the field number and add the observation number once you post.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">5</a></sup></p></li>
    <li><span class="mv-guide-when">Once dry</span><h3>Bag it with its label inside</h3><p>Bag one collection at a time. Keep the matched slip with the specimen. If you use a printed label, check its observation number against the slip before sealing the bag.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">5</a></sup></p></li>
    <li><span class="mv-guide-when">Later</span><h3>Sequencing, then back to the record</h3><p>Note the sequencing plate and well on the observation, add the ITS sequence when it arrives, and the GenBank accession once it’s deposited. Now every piece points to every other.</p></li>
  </ol>
  <div class="mv-guide-photo">Photo to come: a field slip in the photo, beside the mushroom</div>
</section>

<section id="breaks">

  <h2>Most mix-ups happen at the same few moments.</h2>
  <div class="mv-guide-grid">
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>The basket</h3><p>Two similar brown mushrooms, loose in the same bag, and nothing to tell them apart by evening. Separate containers (a tackle box works well), one collection each.</p></div>
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>Upload order</h3><p>Photos from twenty collections, uploaded in a rush, and one set lands on the wrong observation. The slip in the photo is what lets you catch it.</p></div>
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>The dehydrator</h3><p>Labels slide off, pieces fall through the trays, collections touch, and some mushrooms dry to look nothing like they did. Labels or a fixed order on the rack, space between, and a photo of each tray.</p></div>
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>Bagging in bulk</h3><p>Taking everything off and sorting later. Bag one, label it, then the next.</p></div>
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>The record that never got updated</h3><p>A specimen exists, but the observation doesn’t say so, or a sequence came back and was never added. The <a href="/investigate/collection-check/">Collection Check</a> finds these.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="tools">

  <h2>You don’t need to build any of this yourself.</h2>
  <p><strong>Alan Rockefeller’s tools at dikarya.us</strong> do the label work well, and they’re free. His Herbarium Label Generator takes iNaturalist or Mushroom Observer numbers and prints labels for everything you found on a given day, including the DNA barcode and voucher number fields.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">6</a></sup></p>
  <div class="mv-guide-ext">
    <a href="https://dikarya.us/labels">Herbarium Label Generator<small>Alan Rockefeller · dikarya.us</small></a>
    <a href="https://dikarya.us/voucher-labels">Voucher labels<small>Alan Rockefeller · dikarya.us</small></a>
    <a href="https://dikarya.us/voucher-sync">Voucher sync<small>Reads a voucher number from an iNat photo into a field</small></a>
    <a href="https://www.fundis.org/field-data-slips">Field data slips<small>FunDiS · printable</small></a>
  </div>
  <p>On MycoView:</p>
  <div class="mv-guide-ext">
    <a href="/investigate/collection-check/">Collection Check<small>Finds records missing a step</small></a>
    <a href="/investigate/plate-tagger/">Plate Tagger<small>Writes the sequencing plate onto observations</small></a>
    <a href="/investigate/field/drying/">Drying &amp; storage<small>Keeping collections apart on the rack</small></a>
  </div>
  <h3 style="margin-top:18px">How I keep things matched</h3>
  <div class="mv-guide-grid">
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>In the field</h3><p>Tackle boxes, one collection per compartment. I almost never have a mix-up at this stage.</p></div>
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>On the dehydrator</h3><p>Laid out in the order I made the observations, starting at 12 o’clock, with a photo of every tray, because some mushrooms dry to look very different.</p></div>
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>When in doubt</h3><p>If I can’t confidently match a dried mushroom to its collection, it’s discarded, or I make a new observation of it in the lab and note what happened.</p></div>
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>Next season</h3><p>I’m adding voucher slips, using Alan Rockefeller’s voucher slip generator, so a number travels with each collection from the start.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

</FieldGuide>
